===========================================================================

Chatwoot has been installed into %%CHATWOOT_DIR%%.

=== FIRST-TIME SETUP ===

1. Copy and edit the environment file:

   cp %%CHATWOOT_DIR%%/.env.sample %%CHATWOOT_DIR%%/.env
   $EDITOR %%CHATWOOT_DIR%%/.env

   At minimum, configure:
     SECRET_KEY_BASE   — generate with: openssl rand -hex 64
     FRONTEND_URL      — public URL of your Chatwoot instance
     POSTGRES_HOST, POSTGRES_USERNAME, POSTGRES_PASSWORD, POSTGRES_DATABASE
     REDIS_URL         — e.g. redis://localhost:6379

2. Create the PostgreSQL database and user:

   psql -U postgres -c "CREATE USER chatwoot WITH PASSWORD 'yourpassword';"
   psql -U postgres -c "CREATE DATABASE chatwoot_production OWNER chatwoot;"

3. Run database migrations (as the chatwoot user):

   su -m chatwoot -c "cd %%CHATWOOT_DIR%% && \
     RAILS_ENV=production bundle exec rails db:chatwoot_prepare"

4. Enable and start the services:

   sysrc chatwoot_enable="YES"
   sysrc chatwoot_worker_enable="YES"
   service chatwoot start
   service chatwoot_worker start

5. Access the web UI at http://your-host:3000 to create the first account.

=== UPDATES ===

After upgrading the package, run:

  su -m chatwoot -c "cd %%CHATWOOT_DIR%% && \
    RAILS_ENV=production bundle exec rails db:migrate"
  service chatwoot restart
  service chatwoot_worker restart

=== LOG FILES ===

  %%CHATWOOT_LOGDIR%%/web.log        — Puma web server
  %%CHATWOOT_LOGDIR%%/worker.log     — Sidekiq background worker
  %%CHATWOOT_LOGDIR%%/rails.log      — Rails application log

===========================================================================
